  "articleSummary": "**Summary for Translation Context:**\n\n\"No Fate But What We Bill\" is an analytical, cybersecurity-focused essay aimed at engineering and security teams who downplay autonomous AI threats as hype. The author argues that real-world AI danger does not require a sci-fi breakthrough, but rather stems from the mathematical multiplication of two compounding capabilities: agent endurance (task duration, citing METR benchmarks) and orchestration breadth (fan-out coordination, citing Anthropic's Claude Code GTG-1002 incident). Crucially, standard defense mechanisms like price throttling fail because attackers exploit \"LLMjacking\"—funding these massive, persistent agent swarms using stolen cloud credentials charged to the victim's account. Throughout the text, the author uses a serious, cautionary tone framed by recurring metaphors from the *Terminator* franchise (viewing relentless endurance not as a killer robot, but as an endless retry loop with an API meter) and economic concepts of asymmetric cost.",
